# ============================================================================
# Docker Build Context Exclusions
# ============================================================================
# Reduces build time and image size by excluding unnecessary files

# Python Runtime and Cache
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `__pycache__/` MATCHES AT THE CONTEXT ROOT AND NOWHERE ELSE, and this project
# has no `__pycache__` at the root -- so for the whole life of this file the
# line below excluded nothing at all. Measured on the image built 2026-08-20:
# 18 `__pycache__` directories holding 94 `.pyc` files, and three nested
# `.DS_Store`, were inside `/app`.
#
# `**/` IS WHAT CROSSES A DIRECTORY, AND IT DOES NOT CONTRADICT THE
# `filepath.Match` NOTE FURTHER DOWN. Docker does not hand the pattern to
# `filepath.Match` unaltered: it documents `**` as its own EXTENSION to that
# syntax, matching any number of directories including none, precisely because
# `filepath.Match`'s `*` cannot cross a `/`. So the note under "Test Files" is
# still exactly right about a BARE name or a `*` pattern, and `**/x` is a
# different construction rather than a counterexample to it.
#
# THE DOCUMENTATION IS NOT WHAT THIS LINE IS TRUSTED ON. `**` handling has had
# real bugs in real build engines, and a pattern that silently matches nothing
# looks identical to a tree that had nothing to exclude -- which is how the
# root-level line above survived this long. It is trusted because the image was
# rebuilt and the count inside `/app` went 18 -> 0 and 3 -> 0, and because the
# exported context file list lost exactly those 97 files and nothing else.
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
*.pyc
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# Virtual Environments
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
env/
venv/
ENV/
.venv/
venv.bak/

# THE FIVE NAMES ABOVE ARE A NAME LIST, AND A NAME LIST ROTS. Docker matches
# .dockerignore with Go's `filepath.Match` against the path relative to the
# context root, where `*` does not cross a `/` -- so every one of them matches
# a directory called `venv` AT THE ROOT and nothing at any depth. There is no
# marker-based form: `.dockerignore` cannot be told "any directory carrying a
# pyvenv.cfg", which is what `_is_virtualenv` in .github/scripts/static_checks.py
# uses and is the reason that gate survives a venv this file could not name.
#
# `09- Testing/` is that gap, measured rather than argued. It holds nothing but
# `ragas-venv/` -- a real, deliberately UNPINNED environment (see
# oncotriage/evaluation/ragas_harness.py for why ragas is not a pipeline
# dependency), 1.7 GB and 92,649 files -- plus a nested `.DS_Store`. It is
# invisible to git (the venv writes its own `.gitignore` holding `*`), which is
# why `git status` never mentioned it and why nothing here caught it either.
# Docker does not read `.gitignore`, so `COPY . /app/` shipped all of it: the
# image built on 2026-08-20 carried `/app/09- Testing` at 1.8 GB against 7 MB
# for the whole of the rest of /app, and the build context was 1.65 GB.
#
# The WHOLE directory is excluded rather than the venv inside it, because
# nothing in it is needed at runtime -- checked, not assumed: those two entries
# are its entire contents, and the project-root sibling `09- Testing/` that
# holds the characterization fixtures and evaluation runs is OUTSIDE this build
# context and is unaffected. Named explicitly for the reason above: there is no
# pattern that would have caught it.
09- Testing/

# Secrets and API Keys (CRITICAL - never bake into image)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*Keys/
05- Keys/
Keys.txt
*.pem
*.key

